WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.270
It's relatively common for actors to go to
great lengths to inhabit the characters they

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:04.920
portray.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.040
In pursuit of a dynamic performance, they'll
conduct extensive research, learn new skills,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.620
and even alter their appearance.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.300
For some performers, their experiences on
set ended up dramatically altering the course

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.140
of their own lives.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.420
Here are some of the actors whose lives were
permanently changed after they took on a particular

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:21.960
role.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:23.680
Jim Carrey

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.760
To land the role of comedian Andy Kaufman
in 1999's Man on the Moon, Jim Carrey reportedly

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.760
cut his $20 million fee in half and wasn't
too proud to audition for the part.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.000
After he signed on, he went fully method,
living as Kaufman instead of Carrey.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.380
He told the LA Times,

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.480
"I broke a couple of times on weekends and
stuff, but pretty much from when I woke up

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.820
to when I went to bed, the choices were all
[Kaufman's]."

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.860
Carrey's performance would forever change
the way he looked at the world, and led to

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:50.940
an existential crisis.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.600
"When the movie was over, I couldn't remember
who I was anymore.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.100
So you step through the door not knowing what's
on the other side.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.240
And what's on the other side is everything."

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.460
The actor has continued to question the nature
of reality and the meaning of celebrity.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.880
Years later, he was interviewed at New York
Fashion Week, and said,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.580
"There's no meaning to any of this, so I wanted
to find the most meaningless thing that I

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.580
could come to and join and here I am."

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.260
Brie Larson

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:24.020
For the intense 2015 drama Room, Brie Larson
underwent a punishing routine to portray a

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.880
young woman who survives a kidnapping.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:30.640
According to Bustle, on top of restricting
her diet and vitamin intake to match the experience

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:35.190
of her character, Larson also spent six months
of pre-production researching the effects

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.480
of abuse and long periods of confinement.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.380
After shooting the film, Larson emerged as
a powerful voice for survivors of sexual assault.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.960
On the same night she took home the Oscar
for Best Actress, she stood up to hug all

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.120
50 sexual assault survivors who had joined
Lady Gaga on stage for an emotional performance.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.700
In the years since, she's been vocal in opposing
sexual abuse.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.700
She refused to applaud for Casey Affleck's
Best Actor win when presenting him with the

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:05.000
Oscar in 2017, telling Vanity Fair that her
reaction, quote, "speaks for itself,” a

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:09.680
reference to Affleck's legal battles with
two employees who sued him for sexual harassment.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:15.629
According to IndieWire, in 2017 Larson stepped
down from an independent collective she co-founded

00:02:15.629 --> 00:02:19.540
after allegations of sexual misconduct on
the part of some members were made public.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.360
Don Cheadle

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.920
For the premiere of 2004's Hotel Rwanda, Don
Cheadle traveled to Uganda and neighboring

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.560
Rwanda, and the trip certainly made an impact.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:34.120
According to People magazine, after meeting
with genocide survivors, he met with the Rwandan

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.299
President and toured camps in Uganda set up
for refugees fleeing violence at the hands

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.300
of rebel groups.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:44.659
The experience lit a fire inside the actor,
who in 2005 made another trip, this time to

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.150
Sudan, where he visited refugee camps in Darfur.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.940
Cheadle ended up co-authoring a book about
his experiences, called Not On Our Watch:

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.400
The Mission to End Genocide in Darfur and
Beyond.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.620
The book urges U.S. citizens to demand that
their government take action on behalf of

00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.620
imperiled nations.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:04.180
Cheadle is also on the board of a charity
of the same name, an advocacy group cofounded

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.060
by George Clooney.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:06.760
Angelina Jolie

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.680
Though the first Tomb Raider film was popular
among fans of the record-breaking gaming franchise,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.800
it would end up sending leading lady Angelina
Jolie's life in an entirely different direction.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.129
While working on the film and its sequel in
Cambodia, Jolie became aware of the plight

00:03:21.129 --> 00:03:23.700
of the Cambodian people and began relief work
there.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.280
She was named as a UNHCR Goodwill Ambassador
in early 2001.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.980
Less than ten years later, she had visited
more than 20 countries around the world, bringing

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.440
awareness and aid to Sudan, Chad, Syria, and
Iraq, among others.

00:03:37.689 --> 00:03:42.640
In 2005, she launched the National Centre
for Refugee and Immigrant Children, which

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:46.680
provides free legal aid to asylum-seeking
children without representation.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.931
According to ABC News, after being awarded
Cambodian citizenship for her work, Jolie

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:54.840
founded a daycare facility for children with
HIV.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:56.540
Jennifer Garner

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.020
Ben Affleck and Jennifer Garner announced
their plans to divorce in 2015, and when Garner

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.510
was asked how she fared in the aftermath of
the separation, she joked to Vanity Fair that

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.640
it had been, quote, "a year of wine."

00:04:09.029 --> 00:04:12.959
It's not surprising, then, that Miracles from
Heaven, the film that thrust her back into

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.280
the Hollywood spotlight for the first time
since the divorce announcement, was an emotional

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:17.780
experience.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.139
Garner revealed to People magazine that Miracles
from Heaven helped her reconnect to her religion,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:22.640
saying,

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.780
"When I did move to L.A., it wasn't something
that was just part of the culture in the same

00:04:26.789 --> 00:04:30.530
way, at least in my life, so it was a great
gift of this film that it took us back to

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:33.400
finding our local Methodist church and going
every Sunday."

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:35.100
Mary J. Blige

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.879
Musician and actor Mary J. Blige suffered
through a very public separation from her

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:44.419
manager and husband of over a decade, Kendu
Isaacs, and channeled her pain into her first

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.060
starring film role in Mudbound.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.920
The transformation also had a profound effect
on Blige's self-image.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:51.480
She explained to Vulture,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.700
"I had to leave a lot of myself behind, a
lot of Mary J. Blige behind.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:59.460
A lot of wigs, a lot of lashes, a lot of makeup,
a lot of nails, you know, the whole thing."

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:03.420
Blige noted that stripping down to her natural
hair texture was something that she'd always

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:04.420
feared, adding,

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:08.460
"[The role] stripped me down all the way to
what I truly am, and people were complimenting

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:08.960
me.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.560
People were saying how beautiful I was.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.639
I didn't know I was that beautiful for real.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:13.340
[...] I didn't know that."

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:18.340
Her performance earned her two Oscar nominations
for best song and best supporting actress.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.360
Of the new direction her character pushed
her in, Blige noted,

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:22.760
"This is the next chapter."

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:28.880
"It shows that, you know, sometimes we have
to suffer to gain, and I don't mind."

